<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=electric-motor">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Electric motor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An electric motor is a machine that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy, usually through the interaction of a magnetic field and the current in a wire winding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> first showed electromagnetic rotation on 3 September 1821 at the Royal Institution, where a wire carrying a current turned around a magnet in a pool of mercury. Ányos Jedlik built a device with a stator, rotor and commutator in 1828, William Sturgeon a DC motor able to turn machinery in 1832, and Frank Julian Sprague the first practical DC motor in 1886. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> Ferraris and Nikola Tesla invented AC induction motors in 1885 and 1887.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/electric-motor.jpg" alt="Electric motor" width="330" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zureks · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stator_and_rotor_by_Zureks.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1821</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Royal Institution</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Andrew Gordon and Benjamin Franklin described electrostatic motors in the 1740s and 1750s, but these were never put to practical use; Faraday's device of 1821 was the first electromagnetic one.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Electric battery<span>1800</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=electric-motor">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
